# Every key the app requires, listed here — this file is the single source of truth
# for "what configuration does this project need."
#
# mulch runs entirely in the browser: no server, no API keys, no configuration.
# There is nothing to set, which is why `./scripts/dev` needs no wrapper.
#
# ── When the first secret arrives ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Never write a plaintext .env. Values here are 1Password secret references, not
# secrets — a reference is just an address, so this file stays safe to commit and
# safe for an agent to read:
#
#   EXAMPLE_API_KEY=op://mulch/Example API/credential
#
# Then wrap the command in `./scripts/dev` with `op run --env-file=.env.example --`.
# That script is the only place the run command is written down; never copy the
# longhand form into README or AGENTS.md, because those copies drift.
#
# `op run` decrypts into the child process's environment, masks any secret that
# reaches stdout, and the values vanish when the process exits. Nothing touches disk.
#
#   brew install 1password-cli        # then enable Developer → CLI in the app
#   op signin
#   op vault create mulch             # one vault per project, so access is scoped
#
# Verify a reference with `op read '<ref>'` in your own shell — not through an
# agent, which would capture the plaintext in its transcript.
#
# Non-secrets stay literal; only secrets need a reference.
